"# Final Report: Concurrency Analysis of T1, T2, T3 on {A, B}\n\n## Workload\n\n| Txn | Reads | Writes | Role |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| T1 | {A, B} | {A, B} | Read–modify–write |\n| T2 | {A, B} | {A, B} | Read–modify–write |\n| T3 | {A, B} | ∅ | Read-only |\n\n## Isolation Level\n\n- **Design assumption:** Snapshot Isolation with MVCC (snapshot reads, commit-time validation).\n- **History as written (T1 and T2 both commit):** inconsistent with correct SI — it requires a *weak/degraded* SI without first-committer-wins, behaving at roughly Read-Committed/Repeatable-Read grade (Adya PL-2 to PL-2.5).\n\n## Serialization Graph\n\n- Nodes: {T0, T1, T2, T3}; edges from wr (T0→T1, T0→T2, writer→T3), ww (T1→T2 on A and B), and rw antidependencies (T2→T1 on A and B).\n- Contains the directed cycle **T1 →(ww A,B) T2 →(rw A,B) T1** — a **G-single** cycle, interleaving-invariant for two concurrent RMW writers over the same items.\n\n## Anomalies\n\n| Anomaly | Finding |\n|---|---|\n| **Lost update** | ⛔ **Present** (H-literal): both writers read A₀/B₀, both commit; T2's stale-write clobbers T1's updates to A and B |\n| Dirty read | Absent — all reads return committed versions |\n| Non-repeatable read | Absent — one fixed snapshot per transaction |\n| Phantom | N/A — point reads only, no predicates |\n| Write skew | Absent — write sets fully overlap ({A,B} ∩ {A,B}), so the pattern is lost-update, not skew |\n| Fractured read (T3) | Absent — T3 reads both items from one consistent snapshot |\n\n## Serialization Result\n\n- **H-literal (both writers commit): NOT serializable.** The G-single cycle T1 ⇄ T2 admits no equivalent serial schedule — all three orderings of {T1, T2, T3} violate at least one graph edge; the lost update is the outcome-level proof.\n- **Correct SI (first-committer-wins): serializable.** T2's commit fails WW validation (WS(T2) ∩ WS(T1) = {A, B}, C1 ∉ snapshot(T2)); the committed projection is equivalent to the serial schedule **T1 → T3** (symmetrically **T2 → T3** had T2 committed first).\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThe stated Snapshot Isolation is only self-consistent if one writer aborts: **a correct SI engine yields serializability as T1 → T3 at the cost of T2's serialization failure**. If T1 and T2 both commit as literally written, the implementation is not enforcing SI, the history is non-serializable, and T1's updates are silently lost."